# OpenTelemetry Collector deployment

This configuration targets Splunk Observability Cloud realm @@REALM_RAW@@ for cluster
@@CLUSTER_NAME_RAW@@ in the @@ENVIRONMENT_RAW@@ environment.

## Generated outputs

- **Helm files:** `helm/Chart.yaml` and `helm/values.yaml` are generated from
  local templates and pin the Splunk OpenTelemetry Collector chart to
  @@CHART_VERSION_RAW@@. Generating these files does not require the Helm CLI.
- **Kubernetes YAML:** `kubernetes/collector.yaml` deploys a Collector
  directly for GitOps or manifest-based deployment. It uses image
  `quay.io/signalfx/splunk-otel-collector:@@COLLECTOR_VERSION_RAW@@`.
- **Standalone config:** `collector-config.yaml` receives OTLP on ports 4317
  and 4318 and exports metrics and traces. Use it with a compatible current
  Splunk Collector distribution. The generated Collector config also upserts
  `k8s.cluster.name=@@CLUSTER_NAME_RAW@@` and
  `deployment.environment=@@ENVIRONMENT_RAW@@` on metrics and traces.

Logs are not exported by this configuration. Application logs require a separate
Splunk Platform or Splunk Cloud HEC destination and index.

## Create the token Secret

Use a Splunk Observability Cloud organization token with ingest scope. Keep the
token out of values files and shell history:

```bash
printf 'Splunk Observability ingest token: '
read -rs SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN
echo
kubectl create namespace @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
printf '%s' "${SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN}" | kubectl -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ create secret generic @@SECRET_NAME_RAW@@ \
  --from-file=splunk_observability_access_token=/dev/stdin \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -
unset SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN
```

The example Secret files contain `REPLACE_AT_DEPLOY_TIME`, not a usable token.

## Validate

Run from this configuration directory:

```bash
python3 /path/to/otel-generate-app-collector-config/scripts/validate_collector.py .
kubectl apply --dry-run=client --validate=strict \
  -f ./kubernetes/collector.yaml
```

A client dry run does not prove compatibility with a target cluster.

## Deploy with Helm

Helm is needed only if you choose this deployment path:

```bash
helm dependency update ./helm
helm upgrade --install @@RELEASE_NAME_RAW@@ ./helm \
  --namespace @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ \
  --create-namespace \
  --wait \
  --timeout 10m
kubectl -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ rollout restart @@HELM_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@
kubectl -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ rollout status @@HELM_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@ --timeout=120s
```

The restart makes reruns pick up external Secret rotations and regenerated
Collector config even when the chart release itself is otherwise unchanged.

## Deploy Kubernetes YAML

Create the Secret first, then run:

```bash
kubectl apply --dry-run=server --validate=strict \
  -f ./kubernetes/collector.yaml
kubectl apply -f ./kubernetes/collector.yaml
kubectl -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ rollout restart @@KUBERNETES_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@
kubectl -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ rollout status @@KUBERNETES_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@ --timeout=120s
```

The server dry run and apply use the current kubectl context. Confirm the
cluster and namespace before running them. The restart is required after
rerunning the workflow with a rotated Secret or regenerated ConfigMap because
existing pods do not reread those values automatically.

## Local kind smoke test

Use this only to prove the generated YAML starts locally and can receive OTLP.
It does not deploy the application scaffold and it does not require Helm.
The Kind cluster name is derived from the configured cluster name so the smoke
workflow is safe for Kind/Docker while the Collector still emits the original
`k8s.cluster.name=@@CLUSTER_NAME_RAW@@` telemetry attribute.

```bash
kind create cluster --name @@KIND_CLUSTER_NAME_SHELL@@
kubectl cluster-info --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@
```

Create the Secret and deploy the generated Collector YAML to the kind context:

```bash
printf 'Splunk Observability ingest token: '
read -rs SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN
echo
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ create namespace @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ apply -f -
printf '%s' "${SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN}" | kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ create secret generic @@SECRET_NAME_RAW@@ \
  --from-file=splunk_observability_access_token=/dev/stdin \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ apply -f -
unset SPLUNK_ACCESS_TOKEN
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ apply -f ./kubernetes/collector.yaml
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ rollout restart @@KUBERNETES_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ rollout status @@KUBERNETES_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@ --timeout=120s
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ get pods,svc
```

Send test telemetry through the Collector Service with the pinned telemetrygen
image. The tag uses a leading `v`.

```bash
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ run telemetrygen-traces \
  --rm -i --restart=Never \
  --image=ghcr.io/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/telemetrygen:v@@COLLECTOR_VERSION_RAW@@ \
  -- traces \
  --otlp-endpoint=@@COLLECTOR_SERVICE_NAME_RAW@@.@@NAMESPACE_RAW@@.svc.cluster.local:4317 \
  --otlp-insecure \
  --service=otel-generate-app-collector-config-smoke \
  --traces=10

SMOKE_METRIC="otel_generate_app_collector_config_kind_demo_$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)"
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ run telemetrygen-metrics \
  --rm -i --restart=Never \
  --image=ghcr.io/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/telemetrygen:v@@COLLECTOR_VERSION_RAW@@ \
  -- metrics \
  --otlp-endpoint=@@COLLECTOR_SERVICE_NAME_RAW@@.@@NAMESPACE_RAW@@.svc.cluster.local:4317 \
  --otlp-insecure \
  --service=otel-generate-app-collector-config-smoke \
  --otlp-metric-name="${SMOKE_METRIC}" \
  --metrics=20 \
  --rate=5
```

Check Collector logs for exporter authentication or delivery failures:

```bash
kubectl --context @@KIND_CONTEXT_SHELL@@ -n @@NAMESPACE_RAW@@ logs @@KUBERNETES_WORKLOAD_REF_RAW@@ --since=5m | \
  grep -E "Exporting failed|Unauthenticated|error exporting" || true
```

No matching log lines after the smoke test means the Collector did not report
an exporter failure. It is still not the same as proving cloud queryability.

If you have a Splunk Observability API-capable token, query the unique metric:

```bash
printf 'Splunk Observability API token: '
read -rs SPLUNK_API_TOKEN
echo
END_MS="$(python3 -c 'import time; print(int(time.time() * 1000))')"
START_MS="$((END_MS - 900000))"
curl -sS -G "https://api.@@REALM_RAW@@.observability.splunkcloud.com/v1/timeserieswindow" \
  -H "X-SF-Token: ${SPLUNK_API_TOKEN}" \
  --data-urlencode "query=sf_metric:${SMOKE_METRIC}" \
  --data-urlencode "startMs=${START_MS}" \
  --data-urlencode "endMs=${END_MS}" \
  --data-urlencode "resolution=1000"
unset SPLUNK_API_TOKEN
```

If Collector logs show HTTP 401 from
`ingest.@@REALM_RAW@@.observability.splunkcloud.com`, the Secret does not
contain an ingest token accepted for this realm. If the API query returns HTTP
401, the query token is not accepted for the Splunk Observability API; that is a
different failure from Collector export.

## Send application telemetry

Configure workloads to send OTLP to the generated Collector Service in
`kubernetes/collector.yaml`. The matching application overlay generated by
this skill records the exact in-cluster endpoint and patches the selected
workload with OTLP/HTTP environment variables.

Use OTLP/gRPC on port 4317 or OTLP/HTTP on port 4318. The default application
overlay uses OTLP/HTTP.
